1
-1:59:59,950 --> 00:00:02,450
Your phone is the reason you have no identity.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:04,450
I came across this line in a Substack essay

3
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:05,730
recently, and honestly,

4
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:06,840
it made me pause.

5
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:08,090
Because think about it,

6
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:10,410
when was the last time you discovered something you

7
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:11,930
genuinely liked without Instagram,

8
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:14,350
YouTube or algorithm recommending it to you?

9
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:16,650
Look around. We are watching the same movies,

10
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:17,870
listening to the same music,

11
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:19,350
wearing similar clothes,

12
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:20,930
visiting the same aesthetic cafés,

13
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:23,340
and sometimes even developing the same opinions.

14
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:25,080
And somehow, we still think we are expressing our

15
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:26,340
individuality.

16
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:28,370
But here is where it gets interesting.

17
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,370
If you don't spend enough time getting to know

18
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:32,730
yourself, you will end up absorbing everyone else's

19
00:00:32,730 --> 00:00:33,980
definition of you.

20
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:36,480
And I think there is a much bigger idea behind this.

21
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:38,680
There is a concept in philosophy called the Culture

22
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:42,030
Industry, introduced by Theodor Adorno and Max

23
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:42,980
Horkheimer.

24
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,220
They argued that mass-produced entertainment could

25
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:47,300
make culture increasingly standardised.

26
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,010
Now think about what algorithms do.

27
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:50,140
You like something once,

28
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,240
and suddenly you are surrounded by more of the same.

29
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:53,540
Your preferences become predictable,

30
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:54,750
your feed becomes familiar,

31
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,050
and slowly, discovering something outside the bubble

32
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:58,400
starts feeling unusual.

33
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,890
But can we really blame algorithms or capitalism?

34
00:01:00,890 --> 00:01:02,740
After all, we also choose to consume what they

35
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,580
offer.

36
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,250
Maybe the real problem is that we have lost the

37
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:06,990
habit of exploring without being told what to

38
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:07,820
explore.

39
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:09,580
And perhaps the most important question isn't

40
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,850
whether the internet is changing our identity.

41
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:12,750
It's this:

42
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,690
if you stepped away from everyone else's opinions

43
00:01:14,690 --> 00:01:16,230
for a while, would you still

44
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:18,220
know what you actually like?
